# We Are a College Without a Building!
Tamil title: நாங்கள் கட்டடமில்லாத கல்லூரி!

We, belonging to many different parties, have gathered here to speak about the farmers' problem. Though each of us works toward a distinct ideal, a common issue has united us. This is an excellent endeavour. I have always wished that such joint endeavours should occur frequently among political parties. They do more than give people an opportunity to exchange views and enable members of one party to understand the aims of another. If it is shown that something is wrong in their course, such cooperation also helps them correct and modify those particular aspects.

The farmer's life is not a matter belonging to one party. It is a public problem, a problem of the country. On matters of this kind, people of all parties joining in a common endeavour is not only a good thing; it also demonstrates the truth that they genuinely care about the life of the nation.

When Omandurar spoke this morning, he stressed two matters: discipline and dignity. It cannot be said that these lie beyond political parties. Discipline and dignity must form the foundation of every party. A dignified approach will not create mistaken impressions about one's underlying ideas. It leaves no room for deliberately finding fault with others or speaking of them with hatred.

Here are a former Chief Minister, an old landed magnate and I, people from many different positions. Many have not failed to make political predictions about this shared platform. They have already begun their conjectures to the limit of their knowledge, and their predictions to the measure of their ability.

"Good heavens! Is Omandurar attending a conference to which Annadurai is going?" says one voice. "Omandurar! Do not join political masqueraders," warns a handbill addressed to him. I had believed Tiruchi to be a place where brave men lived. But these handbills tell us that there are now some whose knees tremble there too. Beneath the handbill printed to warn Omandurar appear the words, "Thus, the people of the town." Yet the name of the press where it was printed appears beside them. The printer would not have asked whether the townspeople had instructed them. He would only have asked the person who came to print it, "How many notices? How much money will you give?" Whether the public had come to print the notice and whether it was really they who objected were immaterial to him. Either supposing that we would not know this truth, or because cowardice prevented them from putting down their own names, they caused "the people" to be printed below it. I am ashamed that nationalism has made them such cowards!

This morning the former Chief Minister spoke of dignity. He insisted that it is essential in political parties. That is a truth which must be accepted. But where that dignity is diminishing, and in which party it is disappearing, I leave to his own reflection.

The conference pavilion is named for Sardar Patel. When Annadurai may enter a pavilion bearing Sardar Patel's name, why should Omandurar not come to a place where Annadurai is present? May I come to a pavilion named for another leader, while he alone must not have that desire?

Observe this closely and our political dignity will become clear.

Why must I go to a place where the leaders of another party participate? Will our principles diminish if I do not go? Yet why do we participate with enthusiasm when a public issue requires a joint endeavour?

We possess dignity and abundant tolerance. Yielding where necessary and labouring by every possible means to remove suffering are our path. If everyone practised this aim, how could partisan quarrels and futile disputes arise in the country? Unfortunately, political dignity of this degree has not blossomed among the other parties; it has disappeared.

Omandurar, however, is an estimable exception, and an exception of notable measure.

While he governed, I spent a week as his prisoner. He was the Chief Minister and I was a prisoner!

The prisoner and the former Chief Minister have assembled here so that each may grasp a bar and pull to relieve the farmers' suffering. There is no other purpose. Even so, a few nationalist comrades here have printed handbills.

I had thought the armour of nationalism enormously strong. Now I see how quickly it rattles apart and melts in agitation!

They are distressed that, merely by joining this one conference, Omandurar might discard his old armour and put on new armour. Alas, those comrades have understood neither Omandurar nor nationalism, and I sympathise with them.

I am not so mad as to imagine that Omandurar will change parties because I take part in this conference. Nor am I a man who has never seen sudden comrades or those who leap from one nest to another. My party is not so constituted that it must drag in everyone it sees. The people know that our movement has grown without such people.

My sole desire is that deeds should accord with ideas. By some means or other, the country's decline must end.

Omandurar, who opened this conference today and offered valuable reflections, painfully revealed how far both the government and society have neglected the farmers. At the same time, he issued a warning: "Farmers! Political parties will easily deceive you. Therefore do not become slaves to any political party."

It is true that this country's political parties keep trying to exploit the farmers' suffering, discontent and anguish in order to win the electoral gamble. That is why he issued a warning to the farmers and, at the same time, to the political parties.

I belong to a political movement. On behalf of the party to which I belong, I assure him that we will not use farmers in pursuit of political gain.

Other political parties may wish to use the farmer in order to profit at elections. Fortunately, we have neither that desire nor that need.

Our entire aim is that a life without discrimination should arise in society, happiness should blossom everywhere, and everyone should live as a human being.

Our work continues precisely so that this condition may become permanent.

We do not belong to political parties that stir up the merchants' distress, intensify resentment among students, display the teachers' anguish, magnify the workers' sorrow and then count the votes.

We are not people who cast nets to catch fish in the political pond.

If you doubt it, look carefully and ask, "Is there a net in our hands?"

Why did he insist that farmers should not become prey to the desires and passions of political parties? The reason is that poor farmers are uneducated. Political parties could therefore beguile their minds with a little effort. It was with this thought that he spoke. But the farming people of this country possess great power to distinguish grain from chaff. They will not fall into the net and become victims so easily.

Though the leaders of many parties have taken part here today, one truth has emerged, a truth that all of them pointed out: the farmer is not content!

In stating this truth, each person showed a different path.

When I consider the farmer's condition, I do not mean only his pain, the hardship he suffers without irrigation, and the want he endures without manure. Nor do I believe that correcting these alone will produce every happiness. The wealth produced by farmers and the yield obtained through their labour are not sufficient to raise the standard of life of our people. Farmers therefore cannot live by trusting in agriculture alone!

For public wealth to grow and national income to prosper, it is absolutely essential that farmers learn other occupations. The former Chief Minister mentioned this, but he did not identify the kinds of work farmers require. Even if he did, the occupations he proposed might differ from those I wish to propose. He might recommend cottage industries in the Gandhian manner, while I might raise the question whether they would be beneficial.

There may be differences of opinion over which occupations are suitable, but there can be no difference of opinion that the great farming population needs work other than agriculture.

The former Chief Minister repeatedly insisted that farmers must fulfil their demands without falling into the net of political parties.

I welcome that. Organisations and associations are essential for poor farmers whose voices cannot sound and who lie in darkness. Above all, associations free of party affiliation and devoted to relieving farmers' suffering are greatly needed. Like the former Chief Minister, I want them to be selfless associations that labour for the farmers' welfare.

At this moment I therefore ask the leader Omandur to establish farmers' associations in place after place. He is exceptionally qualified to accept this good responsibility. He is upright, detached, experienced in the art of governance, himself engaged in cultivation, and acquainted with the people. I want him to shine as the emissary of this good service.

At the same time, I remind him of the warning he gave farmers this morning: "Omandurar! Everyone has confidence in your upright conduct and integrity. Nevertheless, leave Congress and come out to inaugurate this good work." Why do I say this? If he comes forward to work for farmers while remaining within Congress, people like me might, just as he warned, misunderstand him. Many might suspect that he was seeking a new source of strength and a new road for the collapsing Congress organisation. I therefore want him to leave Congress and devote his time to farmers.

It is rare to find a leader like him to understand the farmer's true pain and wipe away the sorrow of farmers who, unlike politicians, lack the ability to express their suffering in public.

That is why I convey this wish to him now.

Even when anyone enters a good endeavour with purity of heart, the present misfortune of this country causes everyone to be regarded with suspicion. In this wicked world, even good people are not spared suspicion! He knows this better than I through his own experience. That is why I wish him to remove his political armour and engage in this worthy service.

He laboured for many years for freedom. The Congress organisation that fought for *swaraj* has also achieved its ideal. He may therefore peacefully devote the rest of his life to suffering farmers. If he undertakes such good work, I assure him that whatever assistance we can offer will always be available. We have no hidden political motive. We do not work to beg for votes. He can therefore trust us. We will always give our wholehearted support to his good work!

I said that our work is not a hunt for political gain. All we desire is the creation of a new society. The old order must disappear, the harms it causes must vanish, and a new life must blossom.

We are opponents of the old order. We demand renewal and a new awakening. Our aim is to remove the moss of antiquity and spread new light through society. But we have not said that everything must be discarded merely because it is old. Weeds that do not accord with reason must be removed from the field; superstitions that waste thought and time must be removed from society. This is what we have been saying. Do we say that sleeping and eating must stop because they are ancient practices? Do we wish the dams of ancient kings to be destroyed because they are old? Do we ask that towering temple gateways, crystal halls displaying ancient greatness, stairways, ruined forts and ramparts, and the artistic skill visible in them be removed? We do not cling to them merely because they are old. But while taking pride in forts and ramparts, we point to their ruined state and ask: "The beautiful fort has collapsed. Why? Because we were dupes!" We do so from a desire to recover our lost greatness.

We who say the fragrance of renewal must spread through the land, and that the moss of antiquity must be removed, do not say that nothing existed in the old days.

A few things did exist then, suited to the needs and facilities of that age. But times are changing now, and we say that our way of life too must change.

The age in which a man carried a spade on his shoulder has changed; the tractor that ploughs the land has arrived.

Yet ask a farmer about the tractor's name or the benefits it provides. He knows less about it than he knows about Mariamman.

This morning Omandurar explained that the field needs manure. Manure is needed for the field to flourish! But do farmers know the importance of manure? The farmer has heard the priest say during worship, "Eat *panchagavya*," after mixing cow urine and dung. Only to that extent has he learnt the importance of cow dung. Has our farmer understood the importance of dung and its value as manure?

Does he know that tricksters used the almanac to make manure go to waste?

When we speak of all this, they try lightly to dismiss us as "godless." Denying God is not our aim. Are we greater than God that we could deny or conceal him? Or is God someone who will disappear because of our speech?

Here is electricity, providing us both light and sound. So much benefit reaches us through a small wire. If God likewise gives us benefits, would we not welcome them?

Here I am speaking. Electricity is carrying my voice so that people far away can hear it. Suppose the current suddenly fails for a few minutes. You will then be unable to hear my voice, and its value will become apparent. Would we say that electricity, which gives us such benefits, should not exist? Are we mad?

Our work is to teach the rural population the new knowledge it needs. The former Chief Minister said that ninety out of every hundred people in our country are uneducated. Is it possible to give formal education to such an enormous uneducated majority? Could it be done even through a lifetime of effort? Many are elderly; many are trapped in family troubles; crores upon crores wander in search of work. In these circumstances, is it possible to buy textbooks for everybody and teach them to read? It is impossible. That is why we carry out the great task of making general knowledge available to them all. We are colleges without buildings!

Our people know a great deal about what they do not need, but show no concern for what they must necessarily know. Ask a farmer about the tractor. Ask, "Do you know the mechanical plough?" He will say that he does not. Show it to him and he will not care to ask where it was made, who invented it or when it was invented.

Yet the same farmer, travelling by train, will see a hill in the distance and a temple tower at its summit. He will touch his cheeks reverently and ask the person beside him which temple it is, which deity resides there and when its festival occurs. I do not cite this to say that his temple enquiry is wrong or should be forbidden.

But why is the diligence shown there not shown toward the mechanical plough?

It is this state of mind that I wish to point out.

Our people have no concern for understanding innovations. Ask a villager, "Where is Omandur? How many children does Omandurar have? Does he have a wife?" He will answer, "I do not know, I do not know." Ask the same villager, "Where is Kailasa?" He will immediately point to the sky as though he has visited and returned. Ask how many children Lord Siva has. As though Siva lived next door, he will say, "Two children: the elder is Vinayaka and the younger Subramanya." Ask him about our province's revenue and expenditure and he will stare blankly. But ask about Chitragupta, who supposedly keeps the accounts of the illusory world, and he will clear his throat and begin to speak.

I am not even asking whether this is needed and that is not. I ask only why we are concerned to learn about things invisible to the eye, but not about matters that benefit us.

If this condition continues, can general knowledge spread and can our people advance alongside other countries? Or is it only possible for us to remain forever as we are?

This morning Omandurar supplied valuable statistics relating to agriculture. They were useful and necessary. But it is not enough for those facts to remain with us. They must endure in every village. Similar statistics should be displayed in public rest houses in every village, so that people may read and understand them during their leisure. What do we see in the present village rest houses? A neem tree, a small stone beneath it, a festival and celebration for that stone! Is this not how time and thought are being wasted? Instead, why should not all those rest houses become adult schools that spread general knowledge, using the money spent on these futile festivals?

Do village people know India's length and breadth? Leave even that aside: do they know the population of Madras Province? Do we have the time or money to buy each of them a geography book, or is this government capable of doing it?

Village exhibitions would therefore be extremely beneficial in explaining these matters and making them people of general knowledge.

But as we undertake all this, we must not forget one thing.

Until now, when village people were asked how many worlds exist, they were accustomed to answering Atala, Sutala and Patala. If we too continue to share that belief, can we explain conditions in Africa, the greatness of America or the distinction of Australia?

Therefore, while spreading general knowledge and displaying pictures of foreign countries, we must use pictures to explain the people who live there, their conditions and the deity they worship.

"This is a picture of America. The people who live here are Christians and they worship Jesus," we must tell our people. At the same time, we must point out that we have three principal deities and thirty-three crore minor deities.

My purpose in saying this is not that the number of gods must diminish. I refer to the time and thought expended upon gods.

Through exhibitions we must awaken the general knowledge of village people. At the same time, we must enable them to digest all the ideas we present. Our village people possess abundant ability to distinguish good from bad. Farmers know well how to test by rubbing, assess by weighing and discover by sifting.

Only by developing their general knowledge through such sound arrangements can the distinction between village and city disappear.

Two worlds exist in today's society, each denouncing and blaming the other. We must not forget this truth: city and village.

Between the two lies a gulf like that between a mountain and a hollow. We must labour to fill it and build a bridge. This is no ordinary task; it is extremely difficult. If Omandurar enters it, we are ready to cooperate with him. He has earned a good name among the people. He has worked within Congress and obtained its benefits. He can therefore come with fresh vigour and cries of victory. He will not hesitate to stand for the farmers and fight, even if nets are cast by political parties seeking to harass him.

Only a good man like him should undertake such a great task.

One day he spoke in the Madras Legislative Council about the conduct of those who rule. Did that speech not cause both storm and breeze among politicians? Did it not provoke many thoughts? Did it not arouse the people's eagerness to know what would happen next? His words possess such nourishment. His knowledge and ability must serve as good manure for this country's poor village people. That is why I said he must leave the old tent. If he thinks he can perform this good work while remaining in Congress, many like me will suspect that this new effort is intended merely to conceal the bad name Congress has acquired. I therefore ask him to shoulder this valuable endeavour himself.

There are many kinds of people in our agricultural sector: those who work directly upon the land; supervisors; those who never cast an eye upon the field but demand accounts of how much paddy and grain have been produced; and "civilised gentlemen" who never leave their cars and roam the city declaring, "The village, that is where I have..." The difference among them is profoundly harmful.

My view is that the land must belong to the genuine cultivators, to those who descend into it and labour. I place this opinion before the former Chief Minister for his consideration.

Land must not be concentrated in abundance in one place while many possess only tiny plots. Such a condition offers no path to agricultural progress. Moreover, modern methods must be introduced into agriculture, and I want the government itself to undertake that effort. The government should select ten villages and establish experimental centres there. Responsibility for administering agricultural equipment should be entrusted to those who truly understand farming. Only then can poor village people obtain agricultural implements and benefit. When responsibility rests with departmental heads, the rule of the great landowners alone continues! Disciplined farmers' associations are indispensable if farmers are to obtain such benefits.

If they make sound agitations with discipline and devotion to duty in order to secure their demands, and if those in power mistake this for opposition and choose repression, we must not fail to condemn it. In Tanjore district, in particular, farmers formed an association. Communists then entered it. What was the result? Corpses in the fields! Blood in the canals! Police rule! Women's screams! Weeping and obscene barbarous rule raised its head and danced.

I do not wish here to decide who was right or where justice lay.

Going among farmers to remove their suffering is not like merely setting off a firecracker; it is as dangerous as entering an explosives factory carrying a torch.

That is why I insist so strongly that the torch must not approach and politicians must not enter.

In this wicked world, however good a man may be, there are always people who attribute stain and blemish to him. Otherwise, when former Chief Minister Omandur Ramasamy said painfully in the Legislative Council that the government's continued course might force him to begin a struggle, would the present Chief Minister, Kumarasami Raja, have mocked him by asking, "What struggle? Will you conduct a satyagraha?" When I saw that reply in the newspapers, it wounded all our hearts. When a former Chief Minister speaks in pain, should the present Chief Minister, who is in a position to remove that pain, ridicule him?

Does this event not show how far those seated upon the throne of power have become domineering rulers? If such lust for domination increases, it must not be allowed to go unchecked. Strength is needed, not violence. Courage and integrity are needed.

The farmer's sweat cannot be described. The government's cruelty and society's lack of compassion are agonising. The farmer rises before the cock crows and returns home after the owl has set out. Neither scorpion nor crab on the path, nor snake nor insect in the field, stops him. Through such labour he accumulates produce. One group uses that produce in the black market, while the rulers responsible for caring for him make him tremble.

When I read production statistics, all my attention was fixed on how much hardship and loss the farming people must have endured to provide so much labour power. We easily state, "So much," and present a statistic. But behind that statistic lie the sweat farmers shed and the blood they poured!

In villages filled with such people, roads are broken, schools absent and medical facilities unavailable. Should these things not change? They must change until the distinction between village and city disappears. Setting out to serve villages without holding this aim in mind will be fruitless.

The amenities of civilisation must extend to the village: a good hospital, an orderly school and sound roads. It is not enough to sing and demonstrate "Nandanar's Hymn." The Nandan of this age will not return to Chidambaram; he may even depart for Moscow!

In Russia, it is said, there is little difference between village and city because all amenities exist there. I remember reading that the revolutionary Lenin lay on his deathbed while his comrades stood sorrowfully around him. Seeing them grieve, Lenin called one of them and asked why they were so distressed. The comrade wept and replied, "You, our guide, lie upon your deathbed. Who will remain to save Russia?" Lenin is said to have answered, "Do not worry. After me, there is electricity."

I cite this because when electrical facilities become fully available, there need be no cause for anxiety in people's lives. Electricity must likewise spread across our entire country and be used through scientific knowledge.

While saying that our people must use scientific knowledge, I wish to add one more thing: they must learn to respect science. Villagers still speak of the ghost at the rest house and the spirit in the travellers' lodge, but never think about the loudspeaker, wireless telegraphy, ships or submarines.

How many nights must the inventors of these scientific devices have tossed without sleep? How long must they have suffered hardship and loss through their arduous labour to bring these devices into existence? The pain they endured, the sleepless nights and the troubles they bore cannot have been slight.

Could the inventor of this ordinary loudspeaker have completed it the moment he conceived it? Many attempts must have ended in failure before one finally succeeded.

But have our people ever thought about the sweat shed by those scientists?

We travel by train and car. We use bicycles and electric lights. We enjoy them all, assuming they arose for our enjoyment, like a daughter-in-law in her mother-in-law's home.

Have we ever reflected on them? Have we ever thought about them?

No! What use is that? We remain at the level of the traditional standing lamp and have not advanced even to the level of an ordinary hurricane lamp. When the electric light here suddenly goes out, how we panic! The agitation affects not only us; even Sankaracharya experiences it!

But have we understood its value and greatness to the degree we ought? No! Not only that: even as we enjoy modern facilities, do we not disparage them?

Is this right? It is very wrong to deride science. If the scientific temper is belittled, how can the people come to value it?

Some cry that science has nurtured ignorance, reached the atomic bomb and multiplied the power of destruction. The amusing part is that the very people who enjoy scientific facilities most are the ones who say it. I ask them: have instruments of destruction existed only now? We read that the Mohanastra, Agniastra and Varunastra of former times were likewise used for destruction. Can anyone point to an occasion when the people suffered without rain, the Varunastra was immediately released, and they received rainwater? They too were used only for destruction.

By denouncing foreign scientists as ignorant, we therefore damage ourselves. Good and evil exist everywhere and in everything. We must honour the true greatness of science.

Science saves so much of our time and labour. Ten years ago, when a road was laid, ten or twelve men pulled a great stone roller like cattle. Now one small machine rolls not one roller but three.

Through science we receive minimum labour and maximum benefit. This is no ordinary matter.

The gain and convenience we obtain from science are therefore immense. At this point you must recognise one thing: scientists are not selfish. They have made the truths they discovered common property throughout the world. Compare their sacrifice with our condition.

Our village physician gives oil for a headache. Ask him, "What oil is this? How did you make it?" He will tell you nothing.

Likewise he plucks and gives you a leaf. Ask its name and he will not tell you. He explains that if the leaf's name is revealed, it will lose its efficacy and the illness will not leave.

Are Western scientists like that?

Suppose this loudspeaker alone had been made and had fallen into the hands of one of our country's selfish men.

"Do you know what this is? It is Lord Siva's grace," he would say. "Here, apply this sacred ash. I shall make Kailasa speak. Do you hear the sound? You do not hear it? You sinner!" In this way that charlatan would have deceived the people and earned crores by now.

Are scientists such people? No! They have given what they discovered to the whole world.

Science made wealth flourish, and the scientist enabled many others to make money from his discovery. But do you think wealth entered his own life? Do you imagine he conducted these researches in comfort, rather than suffering pain, poverty and the misery of having no money?

Between our farmers and those of Western countries, in clothing and appearance, lies a difference as great as that between mountain and hollow.

"They die in fear upon fear; there is nothing they do not fear": such is the condition of our farming people. When will their lives rise? They lack sufficient food and amenities; life itself is a great affliction to them. Yet are our people not better labourers than Western farmers? Why, then, must they live like this? Let us give a little oil to their parched heads, light to their famine-stricken eyes, cloth that their wives can wear with dignity, civilised clothing for the men, and an opportunity for their children to learn a few letters. Why should we not at least labour to make these available? What if we spent upon their lives at least some small portion of the profit we obtain from them?

Should we not provide all this for them? If society too betrays the villagers, who will raise their lives? I do not say that we alone will do it. But can we avoid thinking that these wrongs must be removed, whoever does the work? Who will come forward with that thought?

That is why, on behalf of these voiceless, life-deprived creatures, I call upon our friend Omandurar, who once governed the country, to go forth and fight. When he was Chief Minister, differences of opinion caused us hardship. Even then we said, "How can such a good man do this?" We never said, "It is he; of course he will act that way." When he introduced and enacted measures for social welfare such as the Hindu Religious Endowments Protection Act, and when the bill to abolish the zamindari system was introduced, our movement stood in support of him and raised its voice for what was needed.

Omandurar is a man whose integrity earned him such a good name that even the opposition called him upright.

He received the opposition parties' breathless cry as his reward!

Whenever the law struck us, we asked with a stifled voice, "Even in Omandurar's time?" We never once said, "Omandurar? He would naturally do that. His rule would naturally be like that."

For any political leader to receive the affection and praise of another party is nearly impossible.

Yet Omandurar received that prize.

At a time when weak men sit as rulers, I point out their conduct and tell him that we are always ready to labour for poor village people. I also lovingly express my wish that his service should be devoted to this worthy cause.

(*Dravida Nadu*, 8 April 1951)
